WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:01.439
- So nice.

00:00:01.439 --> 00:00:03.049
- [Kelly] I'm very
excited actually having...

00:00:03.049 --> 00:00:04.000
You are such a great actor,

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.051
and you are so funny--
- Oh, thanks.

00:00:05.051 --> 00:00:06.600
- [Kelly] And I love you,
and everything you do.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:07.441
- Well, thank you, likewise.

00:00:07.441 --> 00:00:08.803
- [Kelly] I'm starting with that.

00:00:08.803 --> 00:00:10.780
- I'm a huge, huge fan of yours.

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:12.842
- Oh, well, you don't have
to say that, but thank you.

00:00:12.842 --> 00:00:13.675
- [Adam] I don't.

00:00:13.675 --> 00:00:14.811
- You didn't!

00:00:14.811 --> 00:00:15.941
So, you have kids,

00:00:15.941 --> 00:00:17.341
so are they obsessed with "Encanto"?

00:00:17.341 --> 00:00:18.317
Have you seen it a ton?

00:00:18.317 --> 00:00:19.150
- I haven't.

00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:19.983
- [Kelly] Oh.

00:00:19.983 --> 00:00:20.816
- And I don't know if they've seen it.

00:00:20.816 --> 00:00:21.649
- [Kelly] What?

00:00:21.649 --> 00:00:23.141
- I don't know my kids very well.

00:00:23.141 --> 00:00:24.067
(Kelly and audience laughs)

00:00:24.067 --> 00:00:25.129
- Oh, are they older or--

00:00:25.129 --> 00:00:25.962
- They're teenagers.

00:00:25.962 --> 00:00:27.067
They're 13 and 15.
- Okay, okay.

00:00:27.067 --> 00:00:29.278
- So, whatever they see and do,

00:00:29.278 --> 00:00:31.758
I have to pry it out of them.

00:00:31.758 --> 00:00:32.591
- [Kelly] Yes.

00:00:32.591 --> 00:00:36.529
- But, we still watch everything together

00:00:36.529 --> 00:00:38.158
and this is right up their alley.

00:00:38.158 --> 00:00:40.009
So, I'm assuming they have seen it.

00:00:40.009 --> 00:00:41.105
- [Kelly] Okay, it's so good.

00:00:41.105 --> 00:00:42.827
- I know, I've heard it's incredible,

00:00:42.827 --> 00:00:44.419
so I do, I have to watch it.
- Yeah.

00:00:44.419 --> 00:00:47.889
Yeah, Lin, it's rude how talented he is.

00:00:47.889 --> 00:00:48.722
- Yes, I know.
- Yeah.

00:00:48.722 --> 00:00:50.487
- [Adam] And she does
look like the character.

00:00:50.487 --> 00:00:52.047
- She does, it was crazy.
- [Adam] I know enough to know

00:00:52.047 --> 00:00:54.119
that that's a good match.
- Yes.

00:00:54.119 --> 00:00:55.559
Your daughter seems very organized though,

00:00:55.559 --> 00:00:58.159
because you posted this
to-do list that she made,

00:00:58.159 --> 00:00:59.188
which I love.

00:00:59.188 --> 00:01:00.599
- [Adam] Yeah,

00:01:00.599 --> 00:01:01.432
that's it.
- First of all,

00:01:01.432 --> 00:01:02.289
my daughter does this.

00:01:02.289 --> 00:01:03.122
- [Adam] She does?

00:01:03.122 --> 00:01:03.955
- [Kelly] She makes lists,

00:01:03.955 --> 00:01:04.788
and if we don't cross them off,

00:01:04.788 --> 00:01:05.689
she's like "We haven't done this yet",

00:01:05.689 --> 00:01:07.359
which is a stall tactic for bedtime.

00:01:07.359 --> 00:01:08.588
- Did she get that from you?

00:01:08.588 --> 00:01:10.940
- Yes, I am annoyingly organized.

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:12.379
- Okay, good, good.
- Yeah.

00:01:12.379 --> 00:01:13.399
- That's good.
- Are you like that?

00:01:13.399 --> 00:01:14.232
- [Adam] No.

00:01:14.232 --> 00:01:15.139
- No?
- No.

00:01:15.139 --> 00:01:15.972
- [Kelly] Where did this come from?

00:01:15.972 --> 00:01:16.867
- Her mother.

00:01:16.867 --> 00:01:17.700
- [Kelly] Okay.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:18.533
- Yeah, no.

00:01:18.533 --> 00:01:19.689
But I admire it,

00:01:19.689 --> 00:01:21.439
and I wish that I had

00:01:22.807 --> 00:01:24.849
half of the brain that she has.

00:01:24.849 --> 00:01:26.147
- Is she still this organized?

00:01:26.147 --> 00:01:26.980
- [Adam] Absolutely.

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:27.813
- Wow.

00:01:27.813 --> 00:01:28.646
- Absolutely.

00:01:28.646 --> 00:01:29.479
- [Kelly] I love that.

00:01:29.479 --> 00:01:30.707
- She's a very organized 13-year-old.

00:01:30.707 --> 00:01:31.827
- [Kelly] I love efficiency.

00:01:31.827 --> 00:01:32.660
- Me too.

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:34.128
- So, wait, that was your
daughter being organized.

00:01:34.128 --> 00:01:35.187
- Yes.
- So, I did find this

00:01:35.187 --> 00:01:36.478
from your son.

00:01:36.478 --> 00:01:38.149
So, this is your son.

00:01:38.149 --> 00:01:38.982
- [Adam Scott] Oh yeah.

00:01:38.982 --> 00:01:39.815
(audience laughs)

00:01:39.815 --> 00:01:41.778
- [Kelly] So, I'm just
gonna go ahead and say it,

00:01:41.778 --> 00:01:43.218
that's scary as hell, man.

00:01:43.218 --> 00:01:44.149
- [Adam] It is scary.

00:01:44.149 --> 00:01:48.450
- If I came around a corner
at this store, I would...

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:49.283
It's awesome.

00:01:49.283 --> 00:01:51.069
- That was at Michaels, I believe, and--

00:01:51.069 --> 00:01:52.069
- Sure, as you do.

00:01:52.069 --> 00:01:52.902
- That's right.

00:01:52.902 --> 00:01:53.735
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:53.735 --> 00:01:57.168
I saw this happening, and
it just warmed my heart.

00:01:57.168 --> 00:01:58.208
(Kelly laughs and claps)

00:01:58.208 --> 00:02:00.689
Because that...
- That's your son.

00:02:00.689 --> 00:02:02.528
- If I could pull it off, I would do it.

00:02:02.528 --> 00:02:05.538
But he did it, and it freaked
people in the store out.

00:02:05.538 --> 00:02:08.136
And I just couldn't get enough.

00:02:08.136 --> 00:02:08.969
It was a proud moment.

00:02:08.969 --> 00:02:10.746
- What I relate to though
is my son dresses up.

00:02:10.746 --> 00:02:11.826
Like even we go out.
- [Adam] Yeah.

00:02:11.826 --> 00:02:13.626
- Like he was Kroopa,
like in Mario Brothers.

00:02:13.626 --> 00:02:15.046
- Oh, really?
- So, he'll wear characters

00:02:15.046 --> 00:02:16.016
to the store.

00:02:16.016 --> 00:02:16.849
- [Adam] Sure.
- Yeah.

00:02:16.849 --> 00:02:17.734
He just thinks it's hilarious.

00:02:17.734 --> 00:02:19.848
Normal clothes is beneath him.

00:02:19.848 --> 00:02:21.466
- Yeah, it's not fun
to wear normal clothes.

00:02:21.466 --> 00:02:22.778
- He doesn't go scary though,

00:02:22.778 --> 00:02:23.611
but that was awesome.

00:02:23.611 --> 00:02:24.444
- That's frightening.

00:02:24.444 --> 00:02:25.277
- If I turned the corner--
- Yes.

00:02:25.277 --> 00:02:27.064
I would definitely be like...

00:02:27.064 --> 00:02:27.897
- And I would be like "I'm sorry".

00:02:27.897 --> 00:02:29.416
- "Who are your parents?"
- "But I love this."

00:02:29.416 --> 00:02:30.635
(Kelly laughs and claps)

00:02:30.635 --> 00:02:32.502
- So, tell everybody what
the new show is about.

00:02:32.502 --> 00:02:34.864
- The new show is about a company

00:02:34.864 --> 00:02:37.056
that has a procedure where you can get

00:02:37.056 --> 00:02:39.776
a little thing inserted into your brain,

00:02:39.776 --> 00:02:41.246
not a big deal.

00:02:41.246 --> 00:02:42.079
(Kelly and audience laughs)

00:02:42.079 --> 00:02:44.944
Where it splits your
life into two sections.

00:02:44.944 --> 00:02:46.102
When you go to work,

00:02:46.102 --> 00:02:48.835
you cross the threshold of your job

00:02:48.835 --> 00:02:51.926
and you have no idea who you
are in the outside world.

00:02:51.926 --> 00:02:53.835
Your whole life is there at work.

00:02:53.835 --> 00:02:56.044
And then, when you leave work and go home,

00:02:56.044 --> 00:02:58.424
you have no clue what your job is.

00:02:58.424 --> 00:03:00.285
You don't know what you do.

00:03:00.285 --> 00:03:01.766
- That's a crazy cool concept.

00:03:01.766 --> 00:03:03.304
- Yeah, it just separates the two.

00:03:03.304 --> 00:03:06.035
So, you can just enjoy
your life out in the world

00:03:06.035 --> 00:03:07.592
and not worry about work.

00:03:07.592 --> 00:03:08.784
And you can just focus on your work

00:03:08.784 --> 00:03:11.753
and not worry about the outside world

00:03:11.753 --> 00:03:13.656
- Are you good at
compartmentalization in life?

00:03:13.656 --> 00:03:15.675
Can you separate when you get home?

00:03:15.675 --> 00:03:16.606
Do you shut off?

00:03:16.606 --> 00:03:18.456
- I think my job is hard.

00:03:18.456 --> 00:03:21.555
It just blurs the boundaries
all over the place.

00:03:21.555 --> 00:03:23.674
And also, for everyone now I kinda feel,

00:03:23.674 --> 00:03:26.155
like with devices and stuff,

00:03:26.155 --> 00:03:28.115
you're always kind of working, right?

00:03:28.115 --> 00:03:29.904
There's no really putting it down,

00:03:29.904 --> 00:03:30.824
unless you lock it away.
- I try not to.

00:03:30.824 --> 00:03:31.664
I do put it down.

00:03:31.664 --> 00:03:32.497
- [Adam] You do, that's good.

00:03:32.497 --> 00:03:33.744
- Yeah, she'll tell you,

00:03:33.744 --> 00:03:35.443
my assistant will literally be like,

00:03:35.443 --> 00:03:37.053
"So, have you seen the emails?"

00:03:37.053 --> 00:03:37.886
- [Adam] Yeah.

00:03:37.886 --> 00:03:40.270
- And I'm like, "Yes, but I
livin' my life right now."

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:41.152
- Yeah.

00:03:41.152 --> 00:03:42.624
Sometimes, I don't even open--
- It's hard to do it.

00:03:42.624 --> 00:03:44.143
- The emails 'cause I don't wanna know.

00:03:44.143 --> 00:03:45.374
- I have so many blue dots.

00:03:45.374 --> 00:03:46.207
- Oh.

00:03:46.207 --> 00:03:47.040
- Yeah.

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:49.481
- Seriously, how many
unopened emails do you have?

00:03:49.481 --> 00:03:52.820
- I feel ashamed, but
around 26,000, I think.

00:03:52.820 --> 00:03:54.220
(audience murmurs)

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:56.540
Look, and a lot of those are liars.

00:03:56.540 --> 00:03:58.890
They're companies that tell
you you can unsubscribe,

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:00.400
you press unsubscribe--
- 100 percent.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:01.570
- You're never unsubscribed.

00:04:01.570 --> 00:04:03.258
They just send you (beep) for days.

00:04:03.258 --> 00:04:04.091
(audience applauds)

00:04:04.091 --> 00:04:06.769
- How many times have you
unsubscribed from things

00:04:06.769 --> 00:04:08.048
and you keep getting them?
- Why lead me on?

00:04:08.048 --> 00:04:09.791
Exactly, they're liars.

00:04:09.791 --> 00:04:10.624
- Total (beep).

00:04:10.624 --> 00:04:11.457
- Yeah.

00:04:11.457 --> 00:04:13.557
I've got about 26 grand emails,

00:04:13.557 --> 00:04:14.677
like 26,000.
- No joke.

00:04:14.677 --> 00:04:15.510
- Grand?
- I think it's right here,

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:16.757
but I think I'm right there with you.

00:04:16.757 --> 00:04:18.077
- It's bad.
- About 26,000.

00:04:18.077 --> 00:04:18.910
- But I do think it's important

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:19.743
that we do learn how to shut off,

00:04:19.743 --> 00:04:21.328
but I also think it's
a really cool concept,

00:04:21.328 --> 00:04:22.197
because it's like,

00:04:22.197 --> 00:04:23.816
being able to actually
live those two lives.

00:04:23.816 --> 00:04:25.848
- [Adam] That's it.
- Without one impacting

00:04:25.848 --> 00:04:27.035
the other is nice.
- That's right.

00:04:27.035 --> 00:04:29.636
Unless the company that's doing it--

00:04:29.636 --> 00:04:31.768
- Is like evil?
- Has some nefarious,

00:04:31.768 --> 00:04:33.728
which, in the show, they may or may not.

00:04:33.728 --> 00:04:35.448
You have to watch to find out.

00:04:35.448 --> 00:04:38.766
But there might be
something sinister going on,

00:04:38.766 --> 00:04:39.906
we don't know.

00:04:39.906 --> 00:04:40.957
- Very exciting, great cast.

00:04:40.957 --> 00:04:42.077
- Yeah, yeah.
- Yeah.

00:04:42.077 --> 00:04:44.306
- John Turturro and Patricia Arquette

00:04:44.306 --> 00:04:45.498
and Christopher Walken.
- Yeah.

00:04:45.498 --> 00:04:46.496
- Yeah, great people.

00:04:46.496 --> 00:04:47.426
- [Kelly] Incredible cast.

00:04:47.426 --> 00:04:49.035
- [Adam] Ben Stiller directed it, so.

00:04:49.035 --> 00:04:50.029
- [Kelly] Oh wow, I didn't know that!

00:04:50.029 --> 00:04:50.862
- Yep.

00:04:50.862 --> 00:04:51.695
- Oh, that's incredible.

00:04:51.695 --> 00:04:52.549
It's a really cool concept.

00:04:52.549 --> 00:04:53.938
I love the fresh ideas.

00:04:53.938 --> 00:04:54.789
- Yeah, yeah.
- That have never been done.

00:04:54.789 --> 00:04:55.622
That's rare.

00:04:55.622 --> 00:04:56.455
- It's like a whole new world
that they built, it's cool.

00:04:56.455 --> 00:04:57.784
- [Kelly] Yeah, very Aladdin.

00:04:57.784 --> 00:04:59.245
Okay, I like it, another Disney reference.

00:04:59.245 --> 00:05:00.078
- That's right.

00:05:00.078 --> 00:05:00.911
- You got it in there.

00:05:00.911 --> 00:05:02.397
Earlier, we saw a picture of your son,

00:05:02.397 --> 00:05:04.336
so here you are as a kid,

00:05:04.336 --> 00:05:05.277
which is adorable.

00:05:05.277 --> 00:05:06.865
(Kelly and audience laughs)

00:05:06.865 --> 00:05:08.384
And now, you remind me of my son.

00:05:08.384 --> 00:05:09.217
- [Adam] Uh-huh.

00:05:09.217 --> 00:05:10.277
- Is it a boy thing?

00:05:10.277 --> 00:05:11.216
Because here's the thing,

00:05:11.216 --> 00:05:13.195
my son will full on look
you right in the face

00:05:13.195 --> 00:05:14.470
while talkin' and gettin' somethin'.

00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:15.303
- Oh, yeah.
- I'm like,

00:05:15.303 --> 00:05:16.136
"You know, I'm sitting here

00:05:16.136 --> 00:05:17.542
and I can see you picking your booger?"

00:05:17.542 --> 00:05:18.375
- [Adam] Yeah.

00:05:18.375 --> 00:05:19.208
- It's awesome.

00:05:19.208 --> 00:05:20.041
- Yeah.
- It's a boy thing, I think.

00:05:20.041 --> 00:05:20.874
- I think you're right.

00:05:20.874 --> 00:05:22.723
- Okay.
- I mean, I think that--

00:05:22.723 --> 00:05:24.697
- I think girls, we're shady
but we'll hide about it,

00:05:24.697 --> 00:05:25.530
we're hiding, yeah.

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:26.363
- Yeah, you still do it.
- Yeah, little girls

00:05:26.363 --> 00:05:27.196
are hidden.

00:05:27.196 --> 00:05:28.029
- But you just have--
- But they're hidden.

00:05:28.029 --> 00:05:28.862
My daughter, I'll catch.

00:05:28.862 --> 00:05:29.852
- You have the decency not to do that

00:05:29.852 --> 00:05:31.452
when the camera comes, right?

00:05:31.452 --> 00:05:32.932
(audience and Kelly laughs)

00:05:32.932 --> 00:05:33.765
- This is awesome though.

00:05:33.765 --> 00:05:34.598
- Yeah.

00:05:34.598 --> 00:05:36.231
I think I thought I was being hilarious

00:05:36.231 --> 00:05:37.711
and not knowing that one day,

00:05:37.711 --> 00:05:39.713
it would be broadcast on
the "Kelly Clarkson Show".

00:05:39.713 --> 00:05:40.633
- You're welcome.

00:05:40.633 --> 00:05:41.481
You're welcome.

00:05:41.481 --> 00:05:42.393
That's what we're here for.

00:05:42.393 --> 00:05:43.663
- Thank you very much.
- Just to make you

00:05:43.663 --> 00:05:44.496
just bring it down.

00:05:44.496 --> 00:05:45.329
- Appreciate it.

00:05:45.329 --> 00:05:47.473
- So wait, what's something
that your parents let you do

00:05:47.473 --> 00:05:49.292
that you'd never let your kids do?

00:05:49.292 --> 00:05:51.321
- Oh, they used to...

00:05:51.321 --> 00:05:52.919
I got really into the
Grateful Dead for a while.

00:05:52.919 --> 00:05:53.752
I don't know if you ever

00:05:53.752 --> 00:05:54.936
got into the Grateful Dead.
- Wow.

00:05:54.936 --> 00:05:56.054
When I was a teenager, it was--

00:05:56.054 --> 00:05:57.425
- I know the type of people
that were into Grateful Dead.

00:05:57.425 --> 00:05:59.804
- Sure, and that was me,

00:05:59.804 --> 00:06:01.365
for two years.
- Yes.

00:06:01.365 --> 00:06:04.624
- And I don't think
they were aware exactly

00:06:04.624 --> 00:06:06.613
what the Grateful Dead entailed,

00:06:06.613 --> 00:06:09.644
but they would drive me
to Grateful Dead concerts.

00:06:09.644 --> 00:06:12.975
They let me take a bus up to
San Francisco with a friend

00:06:12.975 --> 00:06:15.055
to go to a Grateful Dead show.
- Wow.

00:06:15.055 --> 00:06:16.575
- And spend the night in San...

00:06:16.575 --> 00:06:18.255
I mean this was like a while ago.

00:06:18.255 --> 00:06:19.775
This would never happen now, right?

00:06:19.775 --> 00:06:20.826
- I would never allow my kids.

00:06:20.826 --> 00:06:22.066
- Never.
- Yeah, no.

00:06:22.066 --> 00:06:25.226
- I remember my dad
drove us up to a venue,

00:06:25.226 --> 00:06:26.346
and he's like, "Okay guys",

00:06:26.346 --> 00:06:27.815
me and a friend of mine,

00:06:27.815 --> 00:06:29.026
and as soon as he pulled up,

00:06:29.026 --> 00:06:31.075
people surrounded the car

00:06:31.075 --> 00:06:33.157
and was offering to sell him shrooms.

00:06:33.157 --> 00:06:34.117
(audience laughs)

00:06:34.117 --> 00:06:36.255
- And he still was like,
"Go have a good time, son."

00:06:36.255 --> 00:06:37.575
- Was like, "Yeah, have fun,

00:06:37.575 --> 00:06:39.323
"what is this?

00:06:39.323 --> 00:06:40.706
(Kelly laughs and claps)

00:06:40.706 --> 00:06:42.313
It's the Grateful Dead,
it's not a big deal.

00:06:42.313 --> 00:06:43.734
- It is funny.

00:06:43.734 --> 00:06:44.567
I never was home.

00:06:44.567 --> 00:06:46.723
I was on my bike until the sun
went down, I had to be home.

00:06:46.723 --> 00:06:47.556
- [Adam] Same.

00:06:47.556 --> 00:06:48.389
- They never knew where I was.

00:06:48.389 --> 00:06:49.691
- You leave in the morning.
- I would never let my kids.

00:06:49.691 --> 00:06:50.613
- And you're gone all day.

00:06:50.613 --> 00:06:52.694
- Yeah, but maybe the problem is us,

00:06:52.694 --> 00:06:54.705
because I feel like they did all right.

00:06:54.705 --> 00:06:55.538
I'm all right.
- You're fine.

00:06:55.538 --> 00:06:56.852
- I mean I am in therapy,
but I'm all right.

00:06:56.852 --> 00:06:57.685
- Yeah.

00:06:57.685 --> 00:06:59.734
- But I feel like everyone
should be in therapy, by the way.

00:06:59.734 --> 00:07:00.567
- Whether you left home all day or not.

00:07:00.567 --> 00:07:02.951
- Whether you need it at
that specific moment or not.

00:07:02.951 --> 00:07:04.284
But my point is,

00:07:05.323 --> 00:07:06.993
is that maybe we should
give them more freedom.

00:07:06.993 --> 00:07:07.826
I don't know.

00:07:07.826 --> 00:07:08.905
'Cause I had so much
freedom just like that.

00:07:08.905 --> 00:07:09.763
- Me too.
- Yeah.

00:07:09.763 --> 00:07:10.596
Me too.

00:07:10.596 --> 00:07:13.745
I mean and I think if we
did, they would be fine.

00:07:13.745 --> 00:07:14.578
- [Kelly] I know.

00:07:14.578 --> 00:07:16.433
- But it's still hard not--
- What's wrong with us?

00:07:16.433 --> 00:07:17.945
Control freaks, yeah.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:07:17.945 --> 00:07:18.778
- Apparently.

00:07:18.778 --> 00:07:19.611
I don't know,

00:07:19.611 --> 00:07:20.475
I think we just learned
something together, Adam.

00:07:20.475 --> 00:07:21.642
- [Adam] I think you're right.

00:07:21.642 --> 00:07:22.475
(Kelly and audience laughs)

00:07:22.475 --> 00:07:23.308
- Probably won't change though.

00:07:23.308 --> 00:07:24.141
They're not going out on their own.

00:07:24.141 --> 00:07:24.974
- No way.

00:07:24.974 --> 00:07:25.807
No way.

00:07:25.807 --> 00:07:27.692
- So, in "Parks and Rec", you
got to meet Joe Biden, right?

00:07:27.692 --> 00:07:28.564
- Yeah.
- 'Cause I got to meet him.

00:07:28.564 --> 00:07:29.397
He was so nice.

00:07:29.397 --> 00:07:30.230
- He's the coolest.

00:07:30.230 --> 00:07:31.063
- [Kelly] So, what was
your experience like?

00:07:31.063 --> 00:07:31.896
- It was great.

00:07:31.896 --> 00:07:34.811
He came on, he had a role on the show.

00:07:34.811 --> 00:07:36.820
Like he was obviously playing himself,

00:07:36.820 --> 00:07:40.142
but he was so good and such a pro,

00:07:40.142 --> 00:07:42.900
like really good on
camera, knew his lines.

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:45.500
And then we did another
episode where he and Dr. Biden

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:48.132
were both on, or the
First Lady were both on,

00:07:48.132 --> 00:07:50.358
and they were the best, they're so nice.

00:07:50.358 --> 00:07:51.298
- [Kelly] Yeah, they're very sweet.

00:07:51.298 --> 00:07:52.131
- Yeah.

00:07:52.131 --> 00:07:53.398
- Behind the scenes, that's
what I thought was the coolest.

00:07:53.398 --> 00:07:55.889
She was asking me about just life,

00:07:55.889 --> 00:07:57.108
like kids, normal stuff.
- Totally.

00:07:57.108 --> 00:07:59.038
- And I was like, oh, okay,
you're being normal, okay, cool.

00:07:59.038 --> 00:08:00.089
- [Adam] Yeah.

00:08:00.089 --> 00:08:01.118
- And not that you're not
normal, but you know--

00:08:01.118 --> 00:08:03.870
- Yeah, and he's like,
"Adam, how are you?"

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:06.109
And will shake your hand.

00:08:06.109 --> 00:08:07.499
And you're like, oh, okay,

00:08:07.499 --> 00:08:09.648
there's this magic thing here

00:08:09.648 --> 00:08:11.429
where you're making me feel like

00:08:11.429 --> 00:08:12.779
I'm the most important
person in the world.

00:08:12.779 --> 00:08:13.612
- I know, I know.

00:08:13.612 --> 00:08:15.448
And then you start divulging stuff.

00:08:15.448 --> 00:08:16.281
- Yeah.

00:08:16.281 --> 00:08:17.114
- Actually...
- Yeah.

00:08:17.114 --> 00:08:17.947
- And you're like, yeah.

00:08:17.947 --> 00:08:18.780
- Can I tell you about something

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:19.613
that happened to me
when I was a little kid.

00:08:19.613 --> 00:08:20.672
- My childhood, yes.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:08:20.672 --> 00:08:22.096
And he's like, "No, I'm
not actually into that."

00:08:22.096 --> 00:08:23.736
- My Mom let me go see
the Grateful Dead and--

00:08:23.736 --> 00:08:25.135
- [Adam] That's right.

00:08:25.135 --> 00:08:25.968
That's right.

00:08:25.968 --> 00:08:26.801
- Thank you so much.

00:08:26.801 --> 00:08:27.656
- Thank you, Kelly.
- I love you,

00:08:27.656 --> 00:08:28.867
on screen, but I love
you even more in person.

00:08:28.867 --> 00:08:29.700
- Likewise.

